 Jose Lanz stepped out of the shimmer-cage into air that smelled of wet wool, woodsmoke, and animal dung. The year was 1000. The sky hung low and pewter over a valley of mud and thatch, and the cold bit through the layered graphene weave of his 2150 cloak as if the future itself had never been invented.
    
    He was a man of olive-warm skin and dark, deep-set eyes under thick black brows, the kind of face that carried the long memory of Iberian and American soil in its bones. Even here, a thousand years before the first true map of his bloodline could be drawn, his Spanish rolled instinctive and low in his throat when he cursed under his breath. Madre de Dios. The words tasted older than the machine that had thrown him backward through time.
    
    In 2150 the world was a polished knife. Cities floated on carbon lattices above drowned coastlines. The air was clean only because the poor had been priced out of breathing it. Jose had watched his mother die of a lung-rot the histories blamed on a cascade of decisions made across centuries — deforestation, extractive greed, the slow murder of rivers. The archivists of his age had traced one stubborn root to the year 1000: a forgotten valley in what would one day be called the Old World, where a single monastic scriptorium and a warlord’s grain hoard had set a pattern of scarcity and knowledge-gatekeeping that, they swore, still echoed in the ownership of data and water.
    
    Jose had believed them. He had built the cage. He had come back to break the pattern.
    
    He walked among people who smelled of onion and lanolin, whose teeth were brown and whose gods lived in every oak. He spoke carefully, mixing Latin scraps with the rough Castilian that still lived in his mouth like an heirloom. He bribed a scribe with a bead of pure aluminum. He whispered of crop rotation and clean wells. He sabotaged a granary fire before it could start and, on a night of iron frost, he dragged a feverish child to a spring and forced boiled water down its throat while the mother screamed that he was a demon with eyes too dark and skin too smooth.
    
    He changed things. He felt the past bend under his hands.
    
    When the cage finally answered his recall signal and the valley dissolved into white light, Jose expected a rewritten sky. He expected green coasts and open archives and a mother who still laughed with full lungs.
    
    He stepped into 2150.
    
    The floating cities still glittered above drowned streets. The air still carried the same filtered, metallic sweetness. The same corporate sigils burned on the clouds. His mother’s name was still carved on the same memorial wall of the lung-dead.
    
    For three days he raged. Then he opened the deep archives with the clearance only a man who had touched the year 1000 could now hold, and he found the entries that had never been there before.
    
    A strange traveler of southern complexion and precise speech who had taught a valley to boil water and rotate millet. A granary that did not burn. A child who lived and later fathered a line of scribes. Those scribes kept records that, centuries later, fed the first bureaucratic empires. The empires invented property that outlasted kings. Property invented scarcity as a system. Scarcity invented the long chain of extraction that raised the floating cities and poisoned the lungs of the poor. And among the footnotes of the year 1000, one name appeared in a monk’s trembling hand: Iosephus Lanz, who came from nowhere and left the world different.
    
    Jose sat in the quiet of his capsule and understood.
    
    He had not fixed the past. He had invented the problem by trying to solve it. The future he hated existed only because a man from that future had walked into the year 1000 and done exactly what he had done. The timeline had never been broken. It had been waiting for him to complete the circle he had always been part of. Every act of mercy, every bead of aluminum, every boiled cup of water had been a brick in the wall of 2150.
    
    The paradox was not a flaw in time. It was the structure of time. The past was the way it was because the future had already reached back and made it so. He had never been free to change anything. He had only been free to become the cause of the world that sent him.
    
    Outside his window the carbon lattices shone like cold constellations. Jose Lanz pressed his forehead to the
    glass, Spanish prayers half-formed on his tongue, and laughed once — a dry, unbroken sound. There was nowhere
    left to go. The cage sat dark in the corner. The years 1000 and 2150 held each other by the throat, and he was
    the hand that would never let go.
